Found while doing #26, deliberately not fixed there (one ticket, one change; #26 was scoped to lib/Crypt/Age.pm).

#26 gave Crypt::Age::encrypt/decrypt a byte-string check before their in-memory opens, so a caller passing a decoded character string now gets a message naming the cause and the fix instead of perl EINVAL, and perl own "code points over 0xFF" warning no longer fires.

Crypt::Age::Primitives->encrypt_payload and ->decrypt_payload (Primitives.pm, around lines 292 and 358) take a caller string and map it the same way:

    open my $ifh, "<:raw", \$plaintext or croak "Cannot open input string: $!";

so they still have the untreated form of the same defect: perl warns, the open returns false, and the croak reads "Cannot open input string: Invalid argument", which names no cause. Both are public, documented class methods.

Two pieces, matching what #26 did:
  - the same utf8::downgrade($str, 1) check before the open, with a message naming the parameter and the fix
  - POD saying the parameter is a byte string

The output-side opens there write into a lexical the method owns and are not caller-reachable, same as in Crypt::Age.

Rationale for utf8::downgrade over utf8::is_utf8 or a /[^\x00-\xff]/ scan is written out in the comment above the check in Crypt::Age::encrypt; reuse it rather than re-deriving it.
